explanation:
```react
const LanguageContext = React.createContext("English");
function Greeting() {
  const language = React.useContext(LanguageContext);
  return <p>{language}</p>;
}
function App() {
  return <LanguageContext.Provider value="Spanish"><Greeting /></LanguageContext.Provider>;
}
```
Greeting reads Spanish from the provider.

useContext reads the closest matching provider above that component. A nested provider can override the value for its own descendants.

Compare a consumer outside a provider with one inside:
```react
const LanguageContext = React.createContext("English");
function Greeting() {
  const language = React.useContext(LanguageContext);
  return <p>{language}</p>;
}
function App() {
  return <main><Greeting /><LanguageContext.Provider value="Spanish"><Greeting /></LanguageContext.Provider></main>;
}
```
The paragraphs read English, then Spanish. The provider changes only its descendants, not the context fallback.

